'''''Celtis caucasica''''', the '''Caucasian hackberry''' or '''Caucasian nettle tree''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Cannabaceae]].<ref>{{cite web |title=''Celtis caucasica'' Caucasian nettle tree |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/50155/i-celtis-caucasica-i/details |date=2021 |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=19 October 2021 }}</ref><ref name="1_of_1040_Google_Scholar_Hits" >{{cite journal |year=2021 |volume=2 |issue=1 |last1=Sepahvand |first1=Tooba |last2=Etemad |first2=Vahid |last3=Matinizade |first3=Mohammad |last4=Shirvany |first4=Anoshirvan |title=Symbiosis of AMF with growth modulation and antioxidant capacity of Caucasian Hackberry (''Celtis caucasica'' L.) seedlings under drought stress |journal=Central Asian Journal of Environmental Science and Technology Innovation |doi=10.22034/CAJESTI.2021.01.03 }}</ref> It is native to the Caucasus region, Central Asia, and on to the western Himalaya.<ref name="851016-1" >{{cite web |url=http://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:851016-1 |title=''Celtis caucasica'' Willd. |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=Plants of the World Online |publisher=Board of Trustees of the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew |access-date=19 October 2021 }}</ref> Hardy to [[Hardiness zone|USDA zone 5b]], it tolerates poor soils, drought, and nearby paving, and can be used as street tree.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.vdberk.co.uk/trees/celtis-caucasica |title=''Celtis caucasica'' |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=vdberk.co.uk |publisher=Van den Berk Nurseries |access-date=19 October 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.chicagobotanic.org/plantcollections/plantfinder/celtis_caucasica--caucasian_nettle_tree |title=''Celtis caucasica'' Caucasian Nettle Tree |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2021 |website=Chicago Botanic Garden |publisher=Forest Preserve District of Cook County |access-date=19 October 2021 }}</ref> It is a nitrogen-fixer, in symbiosis with the mycorrhizal fungi ''[[Funneliformis mosseae]]'' and ''[[Rhizophagus intraradices]]''.<ref name="1_of_1040_Google_Scholar_Hits" />

